    "https://www.queensu.ca/filmandmedia/faculty-and-staff/job-postings": {
      "title": "Job Postings",
      "description": "Job Postings | Queen's Film and Media Cookie Disclosure Queen's University uses cookies to provide the best possible online experience. Learn about our privacy practices. OK Skip to main content Skip to footer Sign In University Wide MyQueen’sU - SOLUS, MyHR, and more Microsoft 365 Outlook on the web - Email onQ Research–Service Portal: Cayuse and TRAQ Course Reserves Web Proxy Change password / Manage NetID Faculty/School Portals Business - Program Portals Grad Studies – Faculty and Staff Portal Health Sciences - Elentra Law - QLaw Smith Engineering Intranet Film and Media Arts & Science Main navigation Home Undergraduate Down arrow 2026-27 Curriculum Changes Course Offerings 2026-27 How to Apply Undergraduate Program Overview Road Maps Film Centric Courses Academic Guidelines Facilities and Room Locations FILM 395: FILM Internship Student Bursaries Career Services Graduate Down arrow Screen Cultures & Curatorial Studies Call for Applicants Research Areas How to Apply Funding Master's (MA) Program PhD Program Graduate Courses Guidelines and Forms Upcoming Events Events Calendar Connect2 Vulnerable Media Lab People Contact Us Job Postings Arts & Science Job Postings Want to work at Queen's Film and Media? Find out here if there are any positions available to work for the Department of Film and Media. Please see the specific tabs for different positions available. Expand All Collapse All Tenure Track Teaching and Research  Positions Open Collapsible Close Collapsible There are no positions at this time. Term Adjunct Positions Open Collapsible Close Collapsible There are no positions at this time. Post-Doctoral Fellow Positions Open Collapsible Close Collapsible There are no positions at this time. Teaching Fellows Open Collapsible Close Collapsible The Department of Film and Media will be hiring Teaching Fellows for the following Courses: FILM 236 Fall 2026 FILM 311 Fall 2026 FILM 415 Fall 2026 Course descriptions can be found at: https://www.queensu.ca/academic-calendar/arts-science/schools-departments-programs/film-media/#coursestext Please forward your course preferences and other relevant material to Denise Arsenault, include in Subject Line: FILM TF Posting, FILM ***, at arsenaud@queensu.ca by August 4, 2026. Staff and Technician Positions Open Collapsible Close Collapsible There are no positions at this time. Teaching Assistants Open Collapsible Close Collapsible FILM TA Job Postings for 2026-27 Fall 2026: FILM 215 - Science Fiction FILM 113 -  Intro to Media Studies: Cultures and Technologies Winter 2027: FILM 200 - Video Games FILM 210 - Horror Film FILM 114 - Intro to Media Studies: Art and Practices Summer 2027: FILM 260 - Digital Media Theory (Online) Course descriptions can be found at : https://www.queensu.ca/academic-calendar/arts-science/schools-departments-programs/film-media/#coursestext Positions will remain posted until they have been filled (no less than 7 calendar days). TAships will not be offered to candidates in Group B, C, D, E until the qualified candidates in Group A have been exhausted. In addition, we will do our best to match your preference to course offerings. However, please note that the Collective Agreement does not require departments to match your preference. Group A* Qualified graduate student, for whom TAship is granted as part of the funding commitment, in the Department, Program or Faculty in which TAship is offered, or in an interdisciplinary program without TA budget resources. Group B* Qualified full-time graduate student, for whom there is no funding commitment (all unfunded years), i n the Department, Program or Faculty in which TAship is offered, or in an interdisciplinary program without TA budget resources. Group C* Qualified graduate student, for whom TAship will not form part of the funding commitment, in the Department, Program or Faculty in which TAship is offered, or in an interdisciplinary program without TA budget resources. Group D Qualified graduate student, that previously held a TAship or TFship for the Employer. Group E Qualified graduate student, that have not met the criteria as set out in 12.04 A, B, C, or D. Online Courses: The teaching assistants that we are seeking are for the support of online courses. Some training and preparation are needed before the start of the course. Candidates must be prepared to work outside the regular 9-5 workweek and have access to the internet and a computer that meets minimum requirements. Experience with learning management systems (eg onQ) and videoconferencing software (e.g. Zoom or Adobe Connect) would be an asset. Please forward your course preferences and other relevant material to Denise Arsenault, include in Subject Line: FILM TA Posting, FILM ***, at arsenaud@queensu.ca by August 4, 2026. Group A – please indicate course preference. Group B – indicate course preference, submit CV and unofficial transcripts. Group C – indicate course preference, submit CV and unofficial transcripts. Group D – indicate course preference, submit CV and unofficial transcripts. Group E – indicate course preference, submit CV and unofficial transcripts. Film and Media Isabel Bader Centre for the Performing Arts 390 King Street West Kingston, ON K7L 2X4 Canada Tel: (613)-533-2178 Film and Media on Facebook Film and Media on Instagram Web Privacy Queen’s University is situated on the territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabek . © 2026 Queen’s University"
    },
    "https://www.queensu.ca/french/jobs-french-studies": {
      "title": "Employment Opportunities in French Studies",
      "description": "Employment Opportunities in French Studies | French Studies Cookie Disclosure Queen's University uses cookies to provide the best possible online experience. Learn about our privacy practices. OK Skip to main content Skip to footer Sign In University Wide MyQueen’sU - SOLUS, MyHR, and more Microsoft 365 Outlook on the web - Email onQ Research–Service Portal: Cayuse and TRAQ Course Reserves Web Proxy Change password / Manage NetID Faculty/School Portals Business - Program Portals Grad Studies – Faculty and Staff Portal Health Sciences - Elentra Law - QLaw Smith Engineering Intranet Department of French Studies Main navigation Home About Us Down arrow Mission and Strategic Plan Departmental Strengths Employment Opportunities Undergraduate Down arrow > Getting Started in French Studies Degree Programs French & Concurrent Education Study Abroad Certificates in French Studies Departmental Student Council (DSC) Graduate Courses Down arrow Courses 2026-2027 FREN Option Courses French Non-Credit Classes People Down arrow Faculty Staff Research Down arrow Research Overview Completed Theses Contact Us About Us Employment Opportunities in French Studies Employment Opportunities in French Studies Explore the current list of open positions in the Department of French Studies. Faculty Positions Expand All Collapse All No positions currently available. Open Collapsible Close Collapsible Student Positions Expand All Collapse All Graduate Teaching Fellow - FREN 151 Section 02 - Fall 2026 Open Collapsible Close Collapsible FREN 151 - Français intermédiaire, I (Section 02) Teaching Fellow Position Fall 2026 Department of French Studies Queen’s University Kingston, ON K7L 3N6 The Department of French Studies at Queen’s University invites applications from suitably qualified full-time graduate students at Queen’s University interested in teaching an undergraduate language course, FREN 151 (Français intermédiaire, I), during the Fall 2026 term. This in-person course, delivered entirely in French, focuses on developing intermediate-level proficiency at the B1 level across reading, writing, speaking, and listening as well as confidence in key intermediate-level grammatical concepts. The enrolment capacity for this course is 42 students. This is a Fall term appointment from the period of Sept. 1, 2026 to Dec. 31, 2026, with classes in session from Sept. 8, 2026 to Dec. 8, 2026. The University invites applications from all qualified individuals who have native or near-native proficiency in French and demonstrated interpersonal and/or teaching skills, including the ability to collaborate and work with faculty members, staff, and students with diverse backgrounds. Experience teaching French as a second/additional language and experience with best practices in second language acquisition will be considered an asset. Graduate Teaching Fellows are the instructors of record for the course and carry full responsibility. Duties include planning, preparing class materials, teaching, meeting with and/or responding to student queries, grading assignments, and reporting grades. Graduate Teaching Fellows are expected to comply with all Queen’s University Policies. Candidates must be enrolled in a graduate program at Queen’s University. Queen’s University is committed to employment, diversity and inclusion in the workplace and encourages applications from Black, racialized/visible minority and Indigenous/Aboriginal people, women, persons with disabilities, and 2SLGBTQ+ persons. The University will provide support in its recruitment processes to applicants with disabilities, including accommodation that takes into account an applicant’s accessibility needs. If you require accommodation during this process, please contact our Departmental Administrator, Mary Smida at frenadm@queensu.ca , 613-533-6000 extension 78054. Graduate Teaching Assistants and Teaching Fellows at Queen’s University are governed by the Collective Agreement for Teaching Assistants and Teaching Fellows between PSAC Local 901 and Queen’s University. Remuneration will be in accordance with the Collective Agreement, and appointments are subject to funding and enrolment criteria. How to Apply Applications should include: A cover letter that provides: An explanation of interest in teaching the course; A description of any relevant prior teaching experience, if any; A description of relevant interpersonal and teaching-adjacent skills; A self-assessment of spoken and written French proficiency using the CEFR scale; Preference Group (if applicable), see below A complete and current curriculum vitae Transcripts A letter of reference from one (1) referee; and Any other relevant materials the candidate wishes to submit for consideration such as a teaching dossier, student evaluations, etc. The letter of reference by a qualified referee who is familiar with the applicant’s professional experience should be sent separately by the deadline directly to Mary Smida, Departmental Administrator, at frenadm@queensu.ca Applicants are required to indicate their Preference Group (if applicable) as outlined in Article 12.08 in the Collective Agreement for Teaching Assistants and Teaching Fellows between PSAC Local 901 and Queen’s University within their cover letter. Please arrange to have applications and supporting documentation sent directly to: Mary Smida, Departmental Administrator Department of French Studies frenadm@queensu.ca Applications will be received until July 10, 2026. Review of applications will commence shortly thereafter, and the final appointment is subject to budgetary approval. Additional information about the Department of French Studies can be found at https://www.queensu.ca/french/ Please review course descriptions, term and enrolment capacities below. The course description is also available within the Academic Calendar . Course Description FREN 151  Français intermédiaire I  Units: 3.00 This course, offered entirely in French, is designed for non-native French speakers at the intermediate level (approximately B1) seeking to enhance their grammar, reading, listening, writing, and speaking skills. NOTE Students who have completed Grade 12 Core French, International Baccalaureate French, French Immersion, French-as-a-Foreign Language (or the equivalent of these) should take the Placement Test prior to registering for the course to assess for possible placement in FREN 152 or upper-year French courses (200-level and above). Learning Hours: 120 (36 Lecture, 12 Tutorial, 18 Online Activity, 54 Private Study) Requirements: Prerequisite None. Exclusion FREN 150 /6.0. Offering Faculty: Faculty of Arts and Science Course Learning Outcomes: Demonstrate knowledge of the main rules of French grammar. Choose appropriate strategies to improve your understanding of a written or oral text, and evaluate the effectiveness of these strategies. Compose structured descriptive or narrative written texts with correct use of verb tenses, using the indicative and imperative modes. Interpret a literary text by developing reading and analytical strategies. Express your opinion, in writing or orally, on a given issue or on cultural themes covered in class and/or in your tutorials. Express yourself orally in both informal and formal settings. Posted on June 26, 2026 Graduate Teaching Assistant - FREN 231 - Fall 2026/Winter 2027 Open Collapsible Close Collapsible Graduate Teaching Assistant Position for 2026-2027 French Studies In accordance with the collective agreement between Queen's University and Teaching Assistants (PSAC Local 901), applications are invited from qualified individuals for the following teaching assistant position in the Department of French Studies: FREN 231/3.0 Rédaction et style (Fall Term) A presentation of the principles of textual production in French as applied in the production of various types of texts. FREN 231/3.0 Rédaction et style (Winter Term) A presentation of the principles of textual production in French as applied in the production of various types of texts. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- This TA contract combines two courses with expected hours of: 60 hours in the fall term and, 60 hours in the winter term, totalling 120 hours across the fall and winter terms. Some training and preparation is needed before the start of the course.  Candidates must be prepared to work outside the regular 9-5 work week and have access to the internet and a computer that meets minimum requirements.  Experience with learning management systems (e.g. OnQ ) and videoconferencing software (e.g. Zoom or Adobe Connect) would be an asset. The rate of pay is commensurate with the rates specified in the Collective Agreement of Teaching Assistants (PSAC). TAships are filled according to Group Preferences set out in Article 12.04 of the Collective Agreement between Queen’s University and the Public Service Alliance of Canada. In the appointment to TAships within the Bargaining Unit, the Employer shall with respect to graduate students follow the five level preference system outlined below.  No TAships shall be offered to candidates in Group B until the qualified candidates in Group A have been exhausted. No TAships shall be offered to candidates in Group C until the qualified candidates in Group B have been exhausted. No TAships shall be offered to candidates in Group D until the qualified candidates in Group C have been exhausted. TAships may only be offered to candidates in Group E when there remain no qualified candidates in any other Group. First Preference – Group A Is for qualified graduate students for whom the TAship has been granted as part of the funding commitment offered by the Employer registered as: students in a department or program or Faculty in which the TAship will be offered; or students in an interdisciplinary program with TA budget resources. Second Preference – Group B Is for qualified full-time graduate students for whom there is no funding commitment offered by the Employer and who are registered as: students in a department or program or Faculty in which the TAship will be offered; or students in an interdisciplinary program with TA budget resources. Third Preference – Group C Is for qualified graduate students for whom the TAship will not form part of the funding commitment offered by the Employer and who are registered as: students in a department or program or Faculty in which the TAship will be offered; or students in an interdisciplinary program with TA budget resources. Fourth Preference – Group D Is for qualified graduate students that have previously held a TAship or TFship for the Employer. Fifth Preference – Group E Is for qualified graduate students that have not met the criteria as set out in 12.04 A, B, C, or D. What to include in your application : Most recent CV; Unofficial transcripts; A one-paragraph statement of why you want to TA for a particular course, including a statement of relevant experience/courses taken; Self-assessment of your spoken and written proficiency in French using the CEFR Scale ; Statement regarding which applicant group you are in. Please forward your applications to our Departmental Administrator, Mary Smida ( frenadm@queensu.ca ) by end of day on July 15, 2026 . Posted on June 22, 2026. Staff Positions Expand All Collapse All No positions currently available Open Collapsible Close Collapsible - French Studies Kingston Hall, Rm 300 103 Stuart Street Kingston, ON K7L 3N6 Canada Tel: 613-533-2090 Department of French Studies on Instagram Web Privacy Queen’s University is situated on the territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabek . © 2026 Queen’s University"
    },
    "http://www.queensu.ca/philosophy/jobs": {
      "title": "Job Opportunities",
      "description": "Job Opportunities | Department of Philosophy Cookie Disclosure Queen's University uses cookies to provide the best possible online experience. Learn about our privacy practices. OK Skip to main content Skip to footer Sign In University Wide MyQueen’sU - SOLUS, MyHR, and more Microsoft 365 Outlook on the web - Email onQ Research–Service Portal: Cayuse and TRAQ Course Reserves Web Proxy Change password / Manage NetID Faculty/School Portals Business - Program Portals Grad Studies – Faculty and Staff Portal Health Sciences - Elentra Law - QLaw Smith Engineering Intranet Department of Philosophy Main navigation Home About Down arrow Overview Research Equity and Diversity Committees and Policies Department Newsletters Job Opportunities Donate 2026-27 Course Offerings Undergraduate Down arrow Undergraduate Programs Studying philosophy Undergraduate Courses Down arrow Overview Research Resources Undergraduate Life Graduate Down arrow Graduate Programs Down arrow Overview The M.A. Programs The Ph.D. Program Prospective Students Down arrow Overview Admission Requirements Application Procedures Funding Visiting Students Graduate Courses Grad Life and Community Down arrow Overview The Philosophy Graduate Student Association Graduate Colloquium Resources for Current Students People Down arrow Core Faculty Staff Cross Appointed Faculty Political and Legal Thought Faculty Research Fellows, Postdoctoral Fellows, and Visiting Scholars Graduate Students Down arrow Graduate Students: M.A. Graduate Students: Ph.D. Term Adjuncts and Teaching Fellows Emeriti and Retired Faculty Events Down arrow Event Calendar Upcoming Events Departmental Colloquium News Contact About Job Opportunities Job Opportunities Tenured or Tenure-Track Positions No positions currently available. Teaching Positions - Term Adjunct No positions currently available. Postdoctoral Fellowships No positions currently available. Teaching Fellow Positions No positions currently available. Teaching Assistant Positions No positions currently available. Research Assistant Positions No positions currently available. Undergraduate Positions No positions currently available. The Department of Philosophy John Watson Hall, Queen's University Kingston, ON K7L 3N6 Canada Tel: 613-533-2182 Footer menu Donate General Inquiries People Pages About Graduate Undergraduate Web Privacy Queen’s University is situated on the territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabek . © 2026 Queen’s University"
